--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-001-use-strict-rfc-6901-pointer-evaluation.md
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+---
+id: dl-001-use-strict-rfc-6901-pointer-evaluation
+type: decision-log
+title: "Use strict RFC 6901 pointer evaluation"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Services need one interoperable way to address values in JSON documents. JavaScript property access
+would accept non-standard array keys and can confuse a missing value with a valid `null` value.
+
+## Decision
+
+Evaluate pointers strictly according to RFC 6901: a non-root pointer must start with `/`; reference
+tokens decode only `~0` and `~1`; array tokens must be canonical non-negative decimal indices; and
+every traversed object member or array element must exist as an own value.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The function returns any JSON value, including `null` and `false`, while malformed or unresolved
+pointers reliably throw. JSON Patch's `-` array token is not accepted here because it has no value
+to resolve under JSON Pointer.
